<Layout title="Projects">
  <h1>Research Projects</h1>
  <p>A look at the research I've worked on, from graduate school back through my earliest lab experiences.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Hyaluronic Acid Hydrogels for Soft Tissue Regeneration</h3>
    <p>
      In Dr. Kevin Healy's lab at UC Berkeley/UCSF, I engineer in situ,
      semi-synthetic hyaluronic acid hydrogels designed to help the body
      regenerate tissue after complex soft tissue injuries, such as
      rotator cuff tears. The work draws on hydrogel engineering,
      biochemistry, and cell-material interactions to design a therapeutic
      that's effective, safe, and accessible&mdash;regardless of a
      patient's biology or geography. As an inventor on MuscleMatrix,
      I've helped move this research from the bench toward something that
      could reach the clinic.
    </p>
    <a
      class="btn secondary"
      href="https://ipira.berkeley.edu/musclematrix"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Learn more about MuscleMatrix
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <img
      src="/daps-hydrogel.jpg"
      alt="A dual-network hydrogel sample held with forceps in a gloved hand"
      class="card-img"
    />
    <h3>Dual-Network Hydrogels &amp; Fiber-Reinforced Biomaterials</h3>
    <p>
      As an undergraduate researcher in the Directed Assembly of Particles
      and Suspensions lab at Northeastern, I developed a protocol to
      fabricate dual-network hydrogels reinforced with magnetically
      responsive hydroxyapatite fibers for bone graft applications,
      tailoring the composite design to maximize toughness. I characterized
      the mechanical strength of these materials using scanning electron
      microscopy and dynamic mechanical analysis, and ran COMSOL
      Multiphysics simulations of fiber rotation to investigate a
      potential new toughening mechanism. I also manufactured
      freeze-casted, double-layered porous conduits for peripheral nerve
      repair in collaboration with Dartmouth College and Harvard Medical
      School, and wrote a successful grant proposal that funded this work
      through the PEAK Summit Award.
    </p>
    <a
      class="btn secondary"
      href="https://www.dapslab.com/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Visit the DAPS Lab
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Druggable Pathways for Sickle Cell Disease &mdash; Vertex Pharmaceuticals</h3>
    <p>
      As an Exploratory Research Co-op at Vertex, I developed and optimized
      cellular assays to detect transcription factors in potential
      druggable pathways for the treatment of Sickle Cell Disease, running
      pilot experiments to track protein expression profiles over time. I
      used molecular cloning of recombinant vectors to achieve stable
      integration into cell lines of interest, and regularly presented my
      findings and relevant literature to the Exploratory Research group
      and the broader SCD team.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>CRISPR Screening &amp; Automation &mdash; Editas Medicine</h3>
    <p>
      In the Screening Group at Editas Medicine, I screened for the most
      effective sgRNAs across the company's pipeline diseases and
      performed CRISPR-mediated mutations via nucleofection, transfection,
      and viral transduction. I programmed and optimized high-throughput
      screening assays for automated liquid handlers, ran a full
      comparative analysis of the company's Biomek i7 and Biomek FX
      platforms, and used tools like Prism, Tableau, and FlowJo to analyze
      and present data across the screening and broader research
      departments.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Synthetic-Lethal Targets in Osteosarcoma &mdash; UConn Health</h3>
    <p>
      As a Summer Research Fellow at the University of Connecticut Health
      Center, I piloted synthetic-lethal experiments to uncover exploitable
      vulnerabilities in cell signaling pathways and identify novel
      druggable targets in osteosarcoma. I tested inhibitor and siRNA
      combinations on undifferentiated hFOB cells and osteosarcoma cell
      lines (SaOS2 and U2OS) to selectively target tumor cells with
      growth-factor-receptor mutations, using sterile cell culture
      technique alongside PCR and absorbance assays. This work resulted in
      a poster presentation and laid the foundation for the lab's
      subsequent NIH grant proposals.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
